REASONING: To find the new coordinates of point C after a 90-degree counterclockwise rotation around B, we first need to determine the vector from B to C. This vector will be used to rotate point C relative to B. Then, we apply the rotation transformation to get the new coordinates of C.

The vector from B to C is given by $\vec{BC} = (4-4, 4-1) = (0, 3)$. A 90-degree counterclockwise rotation around any point $(x_0, y_0)$ can be represented by the matrix multiplication:

$$\begin{pmatrix}
\cos(90^\circ) & -\sin(90^\circ) \\
\sin(90^\circ) & \cos(90^\circ)
\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}
x \\
y
\end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
0 & -1 \\
1 & 0
\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}
x \\
y
\end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
-y \\
x
\end{pmatrix}
$$

Applying this transformation to the vector $\vec{BC}$ gives us the new coordinates for point C' (which is the image of C):

$$
\begin{pmatrix}
-x \\
+y
\end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
-(-3) \\
+4
\end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
3 \\
4
\end{pmatrix}
$$

Therefore, the coordinates of the image of C after the rotation are (3, 4).

ANSWER: (3, 4)
